Course 2: Dribbling and change of pace (Detailed Research)

Category: Technique
Duration: 30 min
Level: 3/5
What the Course Should Cover

This course focuses on dribbling circuits with direction and speed changes, which are essential for beating opponents in tight spaces. The modern game requires players to overcome opponents in 1v1 scenarios and break opposition lines with on-the-ball actions . A sudden, explosive change of pace can allow players to move the ball into dangerous areas quickly, while an abrupt change of direction can enable players to receive the ball in space and with time .
Key Coaching Points
Ball Manipulation and Close Control

    Maintain close control of the ball at all times to help avoid obstacles, find space easily, and be ready to execute a change of direction

    Use all parts of the feet — inside, outside, sole — to manipulate the ball

    Keep the ball under tight control when dribbling at moderate pace and changing direction to avoid mistakes or running into traffic 

Awareness and Scanning

    Keep your head up and eyes forward when dribbling so it's easier to see obstacles and identify space that may be opening up

    Keep an eye on both the ball and surroundings to adapt to what is going on while keeping the ball under close control

    Anticipate where space will open up by reading teammates' movements and opponents' body positioning 

Executing the Change

    A quick change of direction should be treated like a feint that serves to evade an opponent when under pressure

    Execute a sudden, explosive change of pace to move the ball into dangerous areas quickly

    Accelerate with the ball when executing a change of direction to overcome opponents

    Drive the ball into space as soon as it appears to avoid being dispossessed easily 

Body Positioning

    Adopt a flexible body shape in readiness to change direction or skill at any time

    Use a low centre of gravity to be close to the ball and protect it from opponents

    A relaxed posture helps players move fluidly, whereas a tight and rigid posture makes it more challenging to dribble out of pressure smoothly 

Common Mistakes to Address
Mistake	Why It Happens	Correction
Ball gets away from player during change of pace	Too heavy a touch when accelerating	Keep touches small and controlled; the ball should stay within playing distance
Player runs into traffic	Not scanning ahead	Keep head up and eyes forward to identify obstacles before changing direction
Change of direction is too slow	Not using body feints to deceive opponent	Treat the change of direction like a feint — deceive the opponent first, then explode
Player loses balance during sharp cut	High centre of gravity	Bend knees and get down low; use arms for balance
Sample Drills
Drill 1: Gates Dribbling (Warm-up) — 5 minutes

    Set up a playing area with several gates (pairs of cones) scattered throughout

    Each player has a ball and dribbles through gates to score points

    Cannot score through the same gate twice in a row

    Progression: When dribbling through a gate, practice a skill to change direction, then add a pace change after the gate 

Drill 2: "Pace" and "Change" Command Drill — 8 minutes

    Players dribble freely in an open area at moderate tempo

    When coach calls "Pace", players dribble at moderate speed

    When coach calls "Change", players significantly increase tempo, dribbling at high speed while maintaining control

    Players must execute quick changes of direction to avoid colliding

    Variation: Players work in pairs, passing back and forth. When coach calls "Change," the receiver executes a quick change of direction immediately before receiving the pass 

Drill 3: 1v1 in a Square — 7 minutes

    Set up a 5x5 yard square with 2-3 players, each with a ball

    Players move around inside the square, using:

        Drag backs

        Scissors

        Cruyff turns

        Drag and tap

    Progression: Play 1v1 in the square; the player with the ball after 20 seconds wins 

Drill 4: Dribble Past an Opponent — 5 minutes

    Mark out lines with cones 15m apart

    Players on opposite cones face each other, each with a ball

    They dribble toward each other; once they meet in the middle, they perform a dribbling action to go around one another and continue to the opposite cone

    Variations: Perform with right foot, left foot, outside of foot, and feint to go one direction but dribble in the other 

Drill 5: Small-Sided Game with Gates — 5 minutes

    Teams compete in a long, narrow pitch with gates along the sides

    Teams score by dribbling through gates (cannot score in the same gate twice in a row)

    Encourages dribbling, skills, feints, turns, and changes in direction and pace to create and exploit space 

Progression Variables

    Space: Larger area → smaller area to increase decision speed

    Opposition: No defender → passive defender → active defender

    Speed demand: Moderate tempo → high tempo on command

    Skill complexity: Basic turns → feints and combination moves (V-cuts, Cruyff touch, stepovers)

    Decision-making: Pre-planned actions → reactive to coach's command → reactive to defender's movements

Assessment Indicators

A player has improved when they:

    Can execute a change of pace without losing control of the ball

    Keeps their head up while dribbling and changing direction

    Uses both feet and all surfaces to manipulate the ball

    Accelerates immediately after a change of direction to escape the defender

    Reads the game to identify when to dribble versus when to pass 

